      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The position of the patella was studied prospectively in both knees of 45 consecutive patients (21 male and 24 female patients aged 16-48 years who were competitive [n = 17] or recreational [n = 28] athletes) who had unilateral patellofemoral pain syndrome without symptoms or signs of patellar instability at initial examination. In each knee, standardized anteroposterior, lateral, and tangential radiographs were obtained and six indexes of patellar position (the ratio of the patellar tendon to the greatest diagonal length of the patella, sulcus angles, lateral patellofemoral angle, lateral patellar displacement, patellofemoral index, and knee angle) were measured. When healthy and affected knees were compared, high riding of the patella due to long patellar tendon (patella alta) was the only definite finding in the affected knees. The shape of the intercondylar sulcus and the mediolateral position of the patella were identical in both knees, providing no evidence for patellofemoral incongruence or lateral patellar tilt. Results of this study strongly suggest that idiopathic retropatellar pain is closely associated with patella alta."},
